@lesson ml-framework
Q: Training error is low and validation error is much higher. What is the model doing?
- Underfitting
* Overfitting
- Converging normally
- Suffering from label noise only
> A large generalization gap is the signature of high variance; regularize, simplify or add data.

Q: In the bias–variance decomposition, which term can no model remove?
- Bias²
- Variance
* The irreducible noise \(\sigma^2\)
- None; all can be removed with enough data
> Noise in the targets limits any predictor, however good.

Q: Which actions mainly reduce variance? (select all)
* Adding more training data
* Stronger regularization
* Averaging an ensemble of models
- Using a more flexible model
> Flexibility lowers bias but raises variance; the others reduce variance.

Q: What is the difference between a parameter and a hyperparameter?
* Parameters are learned from data; hyperparameters are chosen before or around training
- They are the same thing
- Hyperparameters are learned; parameters are fixed
- Parameters only exist in neural networks
> Weights are parameters; tree depth, learning rate and regularization strength are hyperparameters tuned on validation data.

@lesson linear-regression
Q: What is the closed-form least-squares solution?
- \(\hat{\mathbf w}=\mathbf X^\top\mathbf y\)
* \(\hat{\mathbf w}=(\mathbf X^\top\mathbf X)^{-1}\mathbf X^\top\mathbf y\)
- \(\hat{\mathbf w}=\mathbf X^{-1}\mathbf y\)
- \(\hat{\mathbf w}=(\mathbf X\mathbf X^\top)^{-1}\mathbf y\)
> Setting the gradient \(-2\mathbf X^\top(\mathbf y-\mathbf X\mathbf w)\) to zero gives the normal equations.

Q: Why does lasso (L1) produce exactly-zero weights while ridge (L2) does not?
* The L1 constraint region has corners on the axes where the optimum often lands
- L1 uses a larger penalty
- L2 is not convex
- Lasso has a closed form
> The diamond-shaped L1 ball touches the loss contours at a corner, setting some coordinates to zero: automatic feature selection.

Q: The least-squares residual vector is orthogonal to every column of \(\mathbf X\).
= true
> The normal equations say exactly \(\mathbf X^\top(\mathbf y-\mathbf X\hat{\mathbf w})=\mathbf 0\).

Q: Linear regression can fit a parabola if you add \(x^2\) as a feature.
= true
> It is linear in the weights, not in the inputs, so nonlinear features give nonlinear fits.

@lesson logistic-regression
Q: What is the gradient of the softmax cross-entropy loss with respect to the logits \(\mathbf z\)?
- \(\mathbf y-\mathbf p\)
* \(\mathbf p-\mathbf y\)
- \(\mathbf p(1-\mathbf p)\)
- \(-\log\mathbf p\)
> Prediction minus one-hot target: the same simple form as linear regression's gradient.

Q: Why don't we train classifiers by directly maximizing accuracy?
* Accuracy is piecewise constant, so its gradient is zero almost everywhere
- Accuracy is always 100% on training data
- Accuracy is not defined for two classes
- It would be too slow to compute
> Cross-entropy is a smooth, convex surrogate that also rewards calibrated confidence.

Q: In PyTorch, `nn.CrossEntropyLoss` expects…
- probabilities after softmax
* raw logits
- log-probabilities after log-softmax
- one-hot vectors as predictions
> It applies a numerically stable log-softmax internally; feeding softmax outputs double-applies it.

Q: Logistic regression's decision boundary \(\mathbf w^\top\mathbf x=0\) is…
* a hyperplane
- a circle
- a parabola
- any shape
> The model is linear in its inputs, so the 0.5-probability boundary is linear.

@lesson knn-nb
Q: What is the "naive" assumption in Naive Bayes?
- All classes are equally likely
* Features are conditionally independent given the class
- Features are Gaussian
- There are only two features
> It lets \(p(\mathbf x\mid y)\) factor into a product of one-dimensional terms.

Q: Why compute Naive Bayes scores as sums of logs?
* To avoid numerical underflow from multiplying many small probabilities
- Logs make the model more accurate
- To make the scores positive
- It is required for Laplace smoothing
> A product of hundreds of probabilities underflows to 0 in floating point; a sum of logs does not.

Q: Increasing \(k\) in k-NN generally…
- increases variance
* gives smoother decision boundaries with lower variance and higher bias
- makes training slower
- has no effect
> Averaging over more neighbours smooths predictions. k-NN has no training step; prediction cost grows instead.

@lesson svm
Q: Which training points determine an SVM's decision boundary?
- All of them equally
* The support vectors, on or inside the margin
- The points farthest from the boundary
- A random subset
> In the dual solution only points with \(\alpha_i\gt0\) contribute: the support vectors.

Q: What is the margin width of a hard-margin SVM in terms of \(\mathbf w\)?
- \(\|\mathbf w\|\)
- \(1/\|\mathbf w\|\)
* \(2/\|\mathbf w\|\)
- \(\|\mathbf w\|^2\)
> The distance from the hyperplane to each margin is \(1/\|\mathbf w\|\), so the full width is \(2/\|\mathbf w\|\).

Q: The kernel trick lets an SVM work in a high-dimensional feature space without computing the features explicitly.
= true
> Predictions only need dot products, which a kernel \(K(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')=\phi(\mathbf x)^\top\phi(\mathbf x')\) supplies directly.

Q: Increasing the soft-margin parameter \(C\)…
* penalizes margin violations more, lowering bias and raising variance
- widens the margin
- adds more support vectors always
- makes the kernel linear
> Large \(C\) fits the training data more tightly; small \(C\) allows more violations for a wider margin.

@lesson trees-ensembles
Q: A node contains 6 positives and 4 negatives. What is its Gini impurity?
= 0.48 ±0.001
> \(1-(0.6^2+0.4^2)=1-0.52=0.48\).

Q: How does a random forest reduce the correlation between its trees?
- By training each tree on the same data
* By bootstrap sampling and considering a random subset of features at each split
- By pruning every tree to depth 1
- By sharing splits between trees
> Lower correlation \(\rho\) makes the \(\rho\sigma^2\) term of the ensemble variance smaller.

Q: In gradient boosting with squared loss, each new tree is fit to…
- the original targets
* the residuals of the current model
- random noise
- the predictions of the previous tree
> The pseudo-residuals \(-\partial\mathcal L/\partial F\) equal \(y-F\) for squared loss: gradient descent in function space.

Q: Lowering the boosting learning rate (shrinkage) usually requires more trees but generalizes better.
= true
> Smaller steps reduce overfitting to each tree; early stopping on validation picks the number of trees.

@lesson unsupervised
Q: PCA's first principal component is…
* the eigenvector of the covariance matrix with the largest eigenvalue
- the feature with the largest mean
- the first column of the data
- the direction of smallest variance
> Maximizing \(\mathbf u^\top\boldsymbol\Sigma\mathbf u\) subject to \(\|\mathbf u\|=1\) gives \(\boldsymbol\Sigma\mathbf u=\lambda\mathbf u\) with the largest \(\lambda\).

Q: Which statements about k-means are true? (select all)
* Each iteration cannot increase the objective
* It can converge to a local optimum, so restarts help
- It always finds the global optimum
* It is a hard-assignment limit of a Gaussian mixture with equal spherical covariances
> Lloyd's algorithm is monotone but local; k-means++ initialization and several restarts are standard.

Q: Distances between far-apart clusters in a t-SNE plot reliably show how different those clusters are.
= false
> t-SNE preserves local neighbourhoods; global distances and cluster sizes are not meaningful.

Q: In EM for Gaussian mixtures, what does the E-step compute?
- New means and covariances
* Responsibilities: soft assignments of points to components
- The number of clusters
- Gradients of the likelihood
> The M-step then re-estimates parameters from those responsibilities.

@lesson metrics
Q: 1,000 transactions contain 10 frauds. A model flags 20 and catches 8. What is its precision?
= 0.4 ±0.001
> 8 true positives out of 20 flagged.

Q: For the same model, what is its recall?
= 0.8 ±0.001
> 8 of the 10 frauds were caught.

Q: ROC-AUC equals the probability that…
* a random positive is scored higher than a random negative
- a random prediction is correct
- the model is calibrated
- precision exceeds recall
> This ranking interpretation makes AUC threshold-free.

Q: A model is calibrated when…
- its accuracy is above 90%
* among predictions of 0.8, about 80% are positive
- its ROC-AUC is 1
- it outputs only 0 or 1
> Calibration is about trusting the probabilities themselves; check it with a reliability diagram.

@lesson time-series
Q: How should you validate a forecasting model?
- Random k-fold cross-validation
* Backtesting: train on the past, test on the following window, then roll forward
- Leave-one-out
- Test on the training period
> Shuffling lets the model peek at the future, which inflates scores.

Q: Why compute rolling-mean features on `y.shift(1)` rather than on `y`?
* So today's feature does not include today's target value
- To make it faster
- Because pandas requires it
- To remove seasonality
> Without the shift, the target leaks into its own feature.

Q: MASE compares a model's error with that of a seasonal-naive forecast. A MASE of 0.8 means…
- the model is 80% accurate
* the model's error is 20% lower than the naive forecast's
- the model is worse than naive
- the error is 0.8 units
> MASE below 1 beats the naive baseline; above 1 loses to it.

Q: Which loss trains a model to predict the 90th percentile?
- MSE
* The pinball (quantile) loss with \(\tau=0.9\)
- Cross-entropy
- Hinge
> The asymmetric pinball loss makes under-prediction 9 times costlier than over-prediction at \(\tau=0.9\).

@lesson recsys
Q: Why do large recommenders use a multi-stage funnel?
* A heavy ranking model is too slow to score every item, so cheap retrieval narrows candidates first
- To make recommendations random
- Because users want fewer items
- To avoid using embeddings
> Candidate generation (two-tower + ANN) trims millions of items to hundreds, which a rich ranker can afford.

Q: The BPR loss for implicit feedback trains the model to…
- predict star ratings exactly
* rank an observed item above a sampled unobserved one
- maximize item popularity
- cluster users
> \(-\log\sigma(\hat r_{ui}-\hat r_{uj})\) optimizes pairwise ranking.

Q: Which problems are specific to recommenders? (select all)
* Cold start for new users and items
* Feedback loops: the model learns only from what it showed
* Position bias in clicks
- Vanishing gradients
> Each requires dedicated handling: content features, exploration, and debiasing.

@lesson bayesian-ml
Q: Bayesian linear regression's posterior mean equals which estimator?
- Ordinary least squares
* Ridge regression with \(\lambda=\alpha/\beta\)
- Lasso
- k-NN regression
> A Gaussian prior on weights gives the ridge solution; the posterior adds a variance on top.

Q: Which uncertainty shrinks as you collect more data?
- Aleatoric
* Epistemic
- Both equally
- Neither
> Epistemic uncertainty comes from lack of data; aleatoric is noise in the data itself.

Q: Exact Gaussian process regression scales as…
- \(O(n)\)
- \(O(n\log n)\)
* \(O(n^3)\)
- \(O(1)\)
> Inverting the \(n\times n\) kernel matrix costs cubic time, which is why sparse approximations exist.

Q: Deep ensembles estimate uncertainty from the disagreement between independently trained networks.
= true
> Train several seeds; where they disagree, the prediction is uncertain. Simple and effective in practice.

@lesson hpo
Q: Why does random search usually beat grid search for the same budget?
* Each random trial tries a new value of every hyperparameter, including the few that matter
- Random numbers are faster to compute
- Grid search cannot handle integers
- Random search uses gradients
> With a grid, most trials repeat the same values of the important parameter.

Q: Learning rates and regularization strengths should be searched on a log scale.
= true
> Their effect spans orders of magnitude, so 1e-4 vs 1e-3 matters as much as 1e-2 vs 1e-1.

Q: What does Hyperband / successive halving do?
- Trains every configuration to completion
* Starts many configurations on small budgets and promotes only the best to larger ones
- Halves the learning rate every epoch
- Removes half of the features
> Multi-fidelity search spends compute on promising configurations.

@lesson causal
Q: Users of feature X retain 20% better. Why can't you conclude X causes retention?
* Engaged users may both adopt X and retain better: a confounder
- Retention can't be measured
- 20% is too small
- Correlation always implies causation
> Only a randomized experiment, or careful adjustment for confounders, identifies the causal effect.

Q: Roughly how many users per group detect a 1-point lift from a 10% conversion rate at 5% significance and 80% power? (nearest thousand)
= 14000 ±1000
> \(n\approx7.85\cdot2\cdot0.09/0.01^2\approx14{,}100\).

Q: Stopping an A/B test the first time the p-value drops below 0.05 inflates the false-positive rate.
= true
> Repeated peeking gives many chances to cross the threshold by luck; use a fixed horizon or sequential tests.

Q: What does CUPED do?
- Doubles the sample size
* Reduces metric variance using each user's pre-experiment data
- Removes outliers
- Randomizes users
> Lower variance means smaller samples detect the same effect.

@lesson exam
Q: You need a strong first model for a tabular churn dataset with 200k rows, mixed feature types and missing values. What do you start with?
- A deep MLP
* Gradient-boosted trees, after a logistic-regression baseline
- k-NN
- A Gaussian process
> GBDTs handle mixed types, missing values and interactions with little tuning; a linear baseline tells you how much they add.

Q: Your fraud model's ROC-AUC is 0.97 but analysts say most flagged cases are legitimate. What explains it?
* With rare positives, even a low false-positive rate produces many false alarms, so precision is low
- ROC-AUC is computed wrongly
- The model is underfitting
- There is too little data
> Look at precision–recall and tune the threshold for the review capacity.

Q: Which techniques give probability estimates you can use for decisions? (select all)
* Logistic regression
* Calibrated gradient boosting (e.g. isotonic or Platt scaling)
- A raw SVM margin
* A Gaussian process classifier
> SVM margins are scores, not probabilities, unless calibrated.

Q: A model trained on randomly split time-series data scores far better offline than in production. Most likely cause?
- The production server is slower
* Leakage of future information through the random split
- Too much regularization
- The metric changed
> Use time-based backtesting so the model never sees the future during validation.

Q: In gradient boosting, decreasing the learning rate from 0.3 to 0.03 should be paired with…
* more trees, chosen by early stopping
- fewer trees
- deeper trees only
- no other change
> Smaller steps need more of them to reach the same fit.
